/*Q10. Write a Java program to calculate the areas of different geometric shapes using method overloading.
Create a class AreaCalculator that contains overloaded methods named area() to calculate the area of different shapes using different
parameter lists, as given below:
• area(int radius) — calculates the area of a circle using the formula 3.14 × r².
• area(int length, int breadth) — calculates the area of a rectangle using the formula length x breadth.
• area(double side) — calculates the area of a square using the formula side x side.
The Area class should perform the following operations:
1. Read the radius of the circle, the length and breadth of the rectangle, and the side of the square.
2. Create an AreaCalculator object.
3. Call the appropriate overloaded area() methods to calculate the three areas.
4. Display the results in the specified format.
Input Format:
The input consists of four lines:
• Radius of the circle (integer)
• Length of the rectangle (integer)
• Breadth of the rectangle (integer)
• Side of the square (double)
Output Format:
After entering all the input, print the areas in the following order, each on a new line:
• Area of the circle — formatted to 2 decimal places
• Area of the rectangle — integer value
• Area of the square — formatted to 2 decimal places
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class AreaCalculator {
    double area(int radius) {
        return 3.14 * radius * radius;
    }
 
    int area(int length, int breadth) {
        return length * breadth;
    }
 
    double area(double side) {
        return side * side;
    }
}
 
public class Area {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int radius = sc.nextInt();
        int length = sc.nextInt();
        int breadth = sc.nextInt();
        double side = sc.nextDouble();
        AreaCalculator calc = new AreaCalculator();
        System.out.printf("%.2f%n", calc.area(radius));
        System.out.println(calc.area(length, breadth));
        System.out.printf("%.2f%n", calc.area(side));
        sc.close();
    }
}
